  "body": "This single PR records Tejasvi Singh Tomar's public C47 bounds and adds a subsequent candidate, unrefereed lower-bound row obtained by applying Tomar's hole-filling idea with our own c47r1 search scripts. It updates `constants/47a.md` and the README table line, retaining the historical bounds.\n\nTomar's paper is [Bounds for the weak type (1,1) constant of the centred maximal operator over squares](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23716077), October 2026, with [v5, Lemma 6.2, Section 6](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23716077) and [supplement](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23716078). The author line reads **Tejasvi Singh Tomar**. Theorem 1.3 gives **C47 >= 1.7027237335**, from his 50-atom rational periodic measure refining Yongxi Lin's weighted lattice. Theorem 1.2 gives **C47 <= 3.59123**, using Lin's kernel comparison principle. These are Tomar's results, with credit to Lin for the underlying constructions and principles. The paper is a preprint, not peer reviewed; the supplied primary sources cite Zenodo rather than an arXiv identifier. This release reproduces Tomar's lower-bound fraction with his unchanged exact checker; it does not rerun his upper interval verification or Lean proofs.\n\nOur additional row is **C47 >= 1.70311*, candidate, unrefereed; further hole-filling on Tomar's measure: the same insertion-and-refinement operation as his hf.py, applied by our own search scripts**. The exact witness ratio is\n\n$$q=\\frac{215956002657486428307163448091951082893881}{126800499112387565339223240212480000000000}=1.7031163455127832639\\ldots.$$\n\nThe display 1.70311 is rounded down. The construction retains the lower half of the preceding doubled-period measure and repeats it at half the vertical period: 50 inherited atoms and nine hole-filling additions, with no further tuning of their frozen rational coordinates or masses. Our own c47r1 search scripts applied Tomar's insertion-and-refinement operation. The finite-periodic rectangle result is Tomar's Lemma 6.2, following Lin's truncation-and-smearing argument. No novelty, optimality or distinct-method claim is made.\n\nThe frozen decision rule selected option (A): an unchanged v5 hf.py run from Tomar's published seed, seed 1103, default settings and no reach override observed a best floating ratio **1.7027237336033003 in 5.9922 CPU-hours**, below the 1.7030 gate. It stopped during the initial 50-atom polish, never inserted atoms, and produced no completed-round accepted output. This bounded floating experiment is unverified as a new bound; it does not show that further execution of his method cannot reach the candidate.\n\nThe release freshly checks all 559 rational witnesses for the 59-atom certificate, reruns all seven semantic corruptions (all reject), and reruns Tomar's unchanged exact checker fetched at the pinned commit. His checker enumerates 33,356 rectangles and reproduces exactly q. The finite truncation-and-smearing check uses two boundary layers and N = 2,147,177, giving a finite ratio 1.7031100000097934… > 1.70311; every smear inequality passes the receipt audit.\n\n**Verification level: L0** for the bound as a whole (definitions: https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/59eeb7097f806da05187b5f234445be3589204d4/VERIFICATION_LEVELS.md)\nOur exact verifier was separately written by the same AI agent, and Tomar's own unchanged exact checker reproduces the same fraction.\n\nThe earlier 117-atom packet was reviewed by GPT-6 Astra (ACCEPT) and Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Science (FLAG; it corrected an atom-pair count). Both then reviewed this release (the 59-atom certificate, proof, table diff and text), and their findings were wording corrections only, concerning the review attribution and the description of the method; those corrections are fixed in this text. Our publish gate requires both reviewers to accept the final bytes; there is no human referee.\n\n**Third-party replay (7 November).** Arhan Canli [replayed both lower-bound rows exactly](https://github.com/teorth/optimizationproblems/pull/321#issuecomment-6702604742) with independent code, using only the measure files, the witness file, Tomar's paper and Lin's README and proof notes; the replay did not read or run Tomar's scripts or ours. It rebuilds Tomar's Section 6 rectangle family from the paper's description, and two separate exact area computations give exactly the fraction q above for our row and, for Tomar's row, a fraction equal to 1.7027237335962937…. All 559 witnesses of our certificate pass Canli's checks. The replay re-derives Lemma 6.2 and finds no gap. It discloses AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic). [Code](https://github.com/arhancanli/quantavane-replays/tree/ea44adcd6d573d1761f2f336e0c195edb440d1c2/c47). The verification level above remains L0.\n\nSupporting material: [packet and reproduction commands](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/tree/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1), [proof](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1/PROOF.md), [certificate](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1/certificates/half59.json), [exact verifier](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1/verify_exact.py), [fresh exact receipt](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1/logs/half59-exact.json), [fresh Tomar replay](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1/logs/release-half59-upstream.log), [release checks](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1/logs/release-checks.json), [hf.py provenance summary](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1/logs/hf-summary.json), [credit](https://github.com/rpinho/math-certificates/blob/3d4cd5574f894d7df2159dfd29d9dd99461cbe1f/c47/round1/CREDIT.md). No certificate artifacts are added to optimizationproblems.\n\nThe live 47a page and main at `87b4ebbd426ff8db597a2004cc45da17903bdcf2` still list Lin's lower 1.6855099933 and upper 3.879. All 56 open PR file lists and patches, and all 62 open-item bodies, issue comments, review comments and review summaries, were checked on 3 November 2026. No open PR changes 47a or its README bound, and no open issue supplies a competing C47 update. C47 appearances in PRs [#235](https://github.com/teorth/optimizationproblems/pull/235), [#281](https://github.com/teorth/optimizationproblems/pull/281), [#290](https://github.com/teorth/optimizationproblems/pull/290) and [#294](https://github.com/teorth/optimizationproblems/pull/294) are unchanged README context. The source audit is scoped, not a global priority finding.\n\nWe sent Tomar a short note to thank him for the measure and hf.py and to ask whether he would prefer to file the continuation himself or receive different credit.\n\n**Models:** GPT-6.1 Sol via Codex (search, certificate, checkers, packaging); Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Science and GPT-6 Astra (reviews); Claude Code with Claude Opus 5.5 (coordination and PR-text revisions).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01LKxc9HjrcmbqijnWGZgV9t\n\n",
